# JUnit4 declaration task: code review round 1

Verdict: **FAIL**. This is the new independent JUnit4 declaration task; previous
reference-census reviews and correction history remain separate.

Reviewed module: tooling/xtask/src/tasks/android_junit4_declarations.rs

Reviewed SHA-256: 9977721ce60cd61ef52c055ac480058c36f321571a3db5eaa3c9f4b9f19f30c6

Review was static and read-only. No Cargo commands, cache changes, repository
edits, or test execution were performed. Only this requested external report
was written.

## Blocking findings

1. **P2 — Java text-block escapes can invent confirmed declarations**
   (lines 528–544). Escapes are handled only for one-character delimiters.
   Java text blocks also honor escapes. In the following valid source, the two
   escaped quote sequences cause the current scanner to close and reopen the
   literal incorrectly. The intervening phantom method then appears as a direct
   annotated method with balanced braces and a supported JUnit import:

   ~~~java
   import org.junit.Test;
   class Suite {
     String text = """
       \"""
       @Test public void phantom() {}
       \"""
       """;
   }
   ~~~

   Handle Java text-block escapes while preserving Kotlin raw-string semantics.
   A regression must require no phantom annotation or method declaration.

2. **P2 — Java enum type shadowing is omitted** (lines 954–967).
   The declaration/shadowing pass recognizes class/interface/object/record,
   but not Java enum types. For
   "import org.junit.Test; class Suite { enum Test { Value } @Test public void actual() {} }",
   the member type named Test is ignored and actual can be marked confirmed.
   Retain the enum type/shadowing fact and leave the annotation binding
   unresolved. Include the analogous org type case for qualified spelling.

3. **P2 — Imported aliases and static types do not participate in conflicts**
   (lines 896–905, 1021–1032, 1123–1135).
   The explicit Test import calculation excludes aliases and static imports,
   while qualified confirmation checks only locally declared org/Test shadows.
   Consequently these known imported-name conflicts can still confirm:

   ~~~kotlin
   import org.junit.Test
   import other.Annotation as Test
   class Suite { @Test fun actual() {} }
   ~~~

   ~~~kotlin
   import other.Container as org
   class Suite { @org.junit.Test fun actual() {} }
   ~~~

   A conflicting Java single static import of Test is also excluded. Account
   for effective imported names when deciding unique binding and qualified
   prefix shadowing. Keep all ambiguous candidates and binding facts unresolved.

4. **P2 — The new mirror line-inequality assertion contradicts its fixtures**
   (lines 1374–1377). Differing file bytes do not imply differing method lines.
   Direct inspection shows DefaultVariantsTest.debugPreferred at lines 33/38,
   but TabbedToolbarTest.tabIsAdded at line 41 in both AOSP and JetBrains files.
   Use these exact pinned line pairs and retain the distinct hashes, unequal
   file bytes, method names and declaration-count assertions. The original
   fixtures must remain unchanged.

